    This is the README file for the GESTAS Model

    GESTAS - Geophysical EcoSystem and TrAnsport Simulator

#------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Contact:

    Georg Umgiesser
    Oceanography, ISMAR-CNR
    Arsenale Tesa 104, Castello 2737/F
    30122 Venezia
    Italy

    E-Mail : georg.umgiesser@cnr.it

#------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Description:

    GESTAS is based on an idea and realization of ESTAS, a transport model
    working on boxes, developed by Ali Erturk. However, the ESTAS model
    is strongly interlinked with the biogeochemical model AQUABC. On the
    other hand, GESTAS is working with different modules, ecological and
    physical. At this point, modules to compute concentration advection
    and water residence time computations are included, as well as the
    ecological models AQUABC, WEUTRO, and BFM.

    GESTAS takes the information about average values in boxes and fluxes
    across sections between boxes. It then simulates the transport of
    scalars and their reaction in case of ecological models. The box and
    section information can be produced automatically through SHYFEM,
    but is not limited to this, as long as the input files adhere to
    the format needed by GESTAS.

    Workflow:

    The workflow consists basically of three steps:

	1) create the boxes.txt file that contains all the
	   information on how the boxes and sections are structured. What
	   is needed is a GRD file that has as its element type the box
	   number that the element is belonging to. Once this has been
	   achieved standard SHYFEM tools like shybas and shyplot can
	   be used to create the boxes.txt file and plot the information.

	2) run SHYFEM to create the variables on boxes and the fluxes
	   between these boxes. The parameters idtbox=time_step must be
	   set. A typical value for a time step could be 1 hour (3600 s).
	   The input to SHYFEM is the boxes.txt file. This file has to
	   be given with boxes='boxfile' in section $name.
	   running SHYFEM produces various files that are named
	   sim.boxes.what.txt where sim is the name of the simulation
	   and what is one of 2d|3d|meteo|vertical|stats|geom|init.

	3) run GESTAS and apply the desired module. Results will be
	   presented in terms of time series of state variables, which
	   can be elaborated and plotted further. The input to GESTAS
	   are the files mentioned above sim.boxes.what.txt.

    The following modules have been implemented:

	conz	simulate concentration transport between boxes. For
		each of the n boundaries, one type of concentration
		is released.  A last concentration n+1 is released on
		all boundaries.

	wrt	water residence (WRT), confinement, and age
		simulation. Given a reset frequency, the WRT and
		confinement is computed. Age is continuously calculated.

	steady	computes the time how long the system needs to reach
		steady state.

	weutro	runs the WEUTRO ecological model (9 state variables).

	aquabc	runs the AQUABC ecological model (32 state variables).

	bfm	runs the BFM ecological model (not yet ready).

    Credits:

	Credits go to Ali Erturk, who had the idea of this type of
	transport model. With him, also the first coupling of
	GESTAS to AQUABC has been realized.

	Credits also go to the OGS team (Donata Mealku Canu, Isabella
	Scroccaro, Celia Laurent, Leslie Aveytua Alcazar) who enabled
	me through partial funding to finish GESTAS and test it with
	data coming from their applications.

#------------------------------------------------------------------------

Directory structure
===================

arc		archive of some files
bin 		binary files and helper routines (both model and tools)
src 		shyfem model files
    aquabc 	contributed model components (ecological models, etc..)
    bfm 	external tools developed by third party (gotm, etc..)
    weutro	obsolete source files
test	 	test cases for unit test and validation
var		various tools and information

Compiling the model
===================

You can compile everything from the SHYFEM directory by running the
command "make". Available commands are:

make		compiles the model
make help	gives help on available make targets
make compile	compiles the model
make clean	deletes object and executable files in all subdirectories
make cleanall	as clean, but cleans also libraries

